      "postDate": "2026-10-02T03:20:40.287Z",
      "content": "<p>i hope to do something like peak --&gt; fragment loss, then fragment --&gt; assemble to molecule; instead of just directly ms/ms --&gt; molecule</p>",
      "votes": 2,
      "replies": [
        {
          "id": 3531843,
          "authorName": "耶✌",
          "postDate": "2026-10-02T17:29:59.180Z",
          "content": "<p>Yes, I'm thinking along the same lines. The end-to-end task of predicting SMILES is just too difficult. Instead, based on the fragment losses and the observed m/z values, we can predict the combinations of lost molecular formulas. Moreover, for classic substructures like benzene rings, the abundance at m/z 91 almost always indicates the presence of a benzene ring in the molecule. We can compile these kinds of prior knowledge into statistics and let the model learn from them.</p>"
        }
      ]
